---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const sciencesCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Anatomy and physiology: the map', note: 'Nursing decisions start with normal structure and function — you cannot recognise abnormal without a firm grip on normal.' },
    { math: '2 · Pathophysiology: the deviations', note: 'Disease is physiology gone wrong in patterned ways: heart failure, sepsis, diabetes each have a signature the nurse is trained to see.' },
    { math: '3 · Microbiology: the invaders', note: 'Infection control rests on knowing how pathogens spread — contact, droplet, airborne — and which precautions break each route.' },
    { math: '4 · Pharmacology: the interventions', note: 'Most nursing actions touch a drug: dose, route, timing, interactions and the monitoring that catches harm early.' },
    { math: '5 · Assessment binds them', note: 'Vital signs, labs and examination are read against all four sciences at once. That synthesis is the clinical judgement nurses are licensed to exercise.' },
  ],
};

const vitalsCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'hr', label: 'heart rate', min: 30, max: 190, step: 2, value: 76, unit: 'bpm' },
    { name: 'sys', label: 'systolic BP', min: 60, max: 220, step: 4, value: 118, unit: 'mmHg' },
    { name: 'dia', label: 'diastolic BP', min: 30, max: 140, step: 2, value: 74, unit: 'mmHg' },
    { name: 'rr', label: 'respiratory rate', min: 6, max: 42, step: 1, value: 14, unit: '/min' },
    { name: 'temp', label: 'temperature', min: 34, max: 41, step: 0.1, value: 36.8, unit: '°C' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'mean arterial pressure', expr: 'dia + (sys-dia)/3', unit: 'mmHg' },
    { label: 'shock index (HR/SBP)', expr: 'hr/sys', unit: '' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/nursing/foundational/the-sciences-behind-nursing">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Nursing is a clinical discipline with its own licensed judgement — and that judgement rests on
    the sciences: anatomy and physiology for the map, pathophysiology for the deviations,
    microbiology for the invaders, and pharmacology for the interventions. The nurse's daily work
    is synthesising all four from what the patient shows.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four sciences and the judgement that binds them" widget="equationStep" config={sciencesCfg} />

  <h2>Vital signs as a dashboard</h2>
  <p>
    The basic observations compress whole body systems into four numbers. Two derived indices show
    why interpretation matters more than the raw digits: mean arterial pressure (DBP + ⅓ of pulse
    pressure) tracks organ perfusion, and a rising shock index (heart rate ÷ systolic pressure)
    can flag compensated shock before the blood pressure visibly crashes. Drag the sliders toward
    a deteriorating patient and watch which index moves first.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="vitals and the indices derived from them" widget="sliderLab" config={vitalsCfg} />

  <h2>Infection control is applied microbiology</h2>
  <p>
    Hand hygiene, precautions, aseptic technique — the entire infection-control playbook is a
    route-based strategy: contact precautions for organisms that spread by touch, droplet for the
    coughed, airborne for the floating. The nurse is the person who actually interrupts those
    routes, dozens of times a shift.
  </p>

  <h2>Pharmacology is where errors live</h2>
  <p>
    Medication administration is the highest-volume risky activity in healthcare — which is why
    nursing drills the "rights": right patient, drug, dose, route, time, documentation. Behind the
    checklist sits pharmacology: knowing that an ACE inhibitor and a potassium supplement are a
    combination to question, not just to pour.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCLEX-RN Test Plan (NCSBN); OpenStax <em>Anatomy and Physiology 2e</em>; Potter &amp; Perry, <em>Fundamentals of Nursing</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
